    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#kertanegara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kertanegara.jpg" alt="Kertanegara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#kertanegara">Kertanegara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:063_Main_Statue,_Joko_Dolog_(39706345204).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anandajoti Bhikkhu from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Majapahit Empire was established in the late 13th century in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, arising from the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#singhasari">Singhasari</a> kingdom after its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kertanegara">Kertanegara</a>, was killed by the rebel Jayakatwang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> in 1292. Its founder was Kertanegara's son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a> (r. 1293–1309), who allied with the Mongol expedition sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> to punish Kertanegara, used it to defeat Jayakatwang, and then turned on the invaders and drove them from the island. Wijaya's political acumen, combined with the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> plains of the Brantas valley and access to trade routes across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java-sea">Java Sea</a>, enabled the new state to secure both agricultural and commercial resources. Early consolidation involved suppressing rebellions by former allies and subordinating rival Javanese principalities, with the capital established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#trowulan">Trowulan</a>. The wider environment favoured maritime expansion, as trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> and the archipelago was increasing. Under Wijaya's successors, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> (r. 1350–1389) and his chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>, the empire expanded through diplomacy, marriage alliances and military campaigns, becoming one of the most powerful maritime states in Southeast Asian history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Majapahit's prosperity rested on a blend of agrarian productivity and maritime commerce. Irrigated wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> cultivation in the river valleys of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a> supported dense populations and yielded surplus for taxation and export. Society was hierarchically structured, with the king and royal family at the apex, followed by nobles, religious elites, merchants, artisans and a peasant majority. Bonded labour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slavery">slavery</a> supplemented agricultural and construction work, including temple and irrigation projects. Maritime trade was central to the empire's wealth, linking ports from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#borneo">Borneo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> and Maluku with international networks reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. Java exported rice and redistributed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> coins circulated widely as currency. Majapahit drew tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> states in rice, spices and forest products, reinforcing central authority while supporting its fleets. Ports such as Tuban and Gresik functioned as hubs of domestic and international exchange, integrating diverse ethnic communities, including resident Chinese and Muslim merchants, into the imperial economy. This dual reliance on land and sea underpinned Majapahit's longevity and regional influence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#gajah-mada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gajah-mada.jpg" alt="Gajah Mada" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gajah_Mada_in_Relief_of_Indonesian_History,_Monas.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gunawan Kartapranata, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Majapahit military was a hybrid force capable of both land and naval operations, reflecting the empire's dual agrarian and maritime character. Land forces comprised peasant levies and elite warrior retainers armed with spears, krisses, swords and bows, and by the later period with small bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">cannon</a> known as cetbang. Elephants and horses were used, mainly by the elite and for ceremonial purposes, rather than as the core of the army. The fleet, crucial to control of the archipelago, included large ships capable of transporting troops and supplies over long distances, enabling rapid responses to revolt. Campaigns directed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>, who according to tradition swore the Palapa oath to unite the archipelago, subjugated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bali">Bali</a> in 1343 and extended Majapahit power over parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#borneo">Borneo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>; the Battle of Bubat in 1357, in which the Sundanese royal party was massacred, showed the coercive edge of this policy. Military organisation was closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> system, with regional lords contributing forces in exchange for recognition and protection. The combination of levied infantry, elite retainers and a capable navy allowed Majapahit to maintain hegemony over much of the archipelago for most of the 14th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Majapahit's governance was centred on the king, whose authority was reinforced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> ideals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#divine-kingship">divine kingship</a>, presenting him as an incarnation of the gods and an intermediary between the human and spiritual realms. Administration relied on a hierarchy of ministers, headed by the chief minister (patih amangkubhumi), together with royal relatives governing provinces and local chiefs who collected taxes, enforced law and mobilised military resources. Law combined customary (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adat">adat</a>) practice with royal decrees and Indian-derived legal codes, recorded in inscriptions and law books, while religious officials for Shaivite and Buddhist affairs played a key role in resolving disputes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">Vassal</a> states retained a high degree of autonomy but were expected to recognise royal authority, send tribute and supply military support. The court's administrative sophistication enabled the coordination of irrigation works, temples and port facilities across a vast maritime network. This structure promoted both political stability and social stratification, allowing Majapahit to integrate diverse ethnic, linguistic and religious communities while projecting influence over distant territories through a combination of central oversight and delegated regional rule.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#trowulan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trowulan.jpg" alt="Trowulan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#trowulan">Trowulan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eksotika_Candi_Bajang_Ratu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ivuvisual, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Majapahit exerted substantial influence over maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, shaping political, economic and cultural patterns throughout the archipelago. Its command of strategic ports facilitated the flow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a>, textiles, gold and forest products, linking regional economies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> and beyond. Majapahit culture, language and administrative practice spread through its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a>, leaving enduring legacies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bali">Bali</a> and the Malay world; Bali in particular preserved its Hindu-Javanese court culture long after Java itself converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a>. The court's patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> traditions fostered artistic and literary achievements, including the temples of Panataran and the brick architecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#trowulan">Trowulan</a>, and Old Javanese works such as the <em>Nagarakretagama</em> of Mpu Prapanca (1365), which describes the realm at its height. Diplomatic engagement, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tributary-system">tributary relations</a> with Ming China, helped stabilise regional trade networks. By projecting a model of maritime supremacy coupled with centralised rule, Majapahit shaped the political imagination of Southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>, and it remains a powerful symbol of archipelagic unity in Indonesian historical memory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#demak-sultanate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demak-sultanate.jpg" alt="Demak Sultanate" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#demak-sultanate">Demak Sultanate</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MUS_Koin_Sultan_Pangeran_Raden_Fatah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mhatopzz, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of the Majapahit Empire began after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> in 1389, accelerated by succession disputes, weakening central authority and the rise of regional rivals. A civil war between the western and eastern courts, the Regreg War of 1404–1406, drained the kingdom's strength. Economic shifts, especially the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malacca">Malacca</a> as the dominant port of the straits in the 15th century and the growing wealth of Muslim trading communities on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, reduced the empire's fiscal and political base. Rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> and ambitious coastal rulers, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#demak-sultanate">Sultanate of Demak</a>, further undermined cohesion. Javanese tradition dates the fall of the capital to 1478, though a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> Majapahit court survived in reduced form, probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>, until Demak's conquest around 1527. The gradual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islamisation">Islamisation</a> of coastal regions eroded the ideological foundations of the Hindu-Buddhist court, and many of its nobles, priests and artists are traditionally said to have withdrawn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bali">Bali</a>. Majapahit's territories were absorbed by the emerging sultanates, illustrating the vulnerability of maritime states to both internal factionalism and shifting trade networks.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary"><em>The Majapahit Empire stands as the high point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, a maritime power that combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a>-growing wealth with command of the archipelago's sea lanes and left a model of statecraft for the sultanates that succeeded it.</em></p>

